<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page2-tennis-other-methods" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Tennis, and Other Ways to Find Mixed Equilibria</title>

  <p>
    On the last page we verified that <m>(.6b + .4f,\ .7b + .3f)</m> is a
    mixed strategy Nash equilibrium. But we want to find <em>all</em> of them.
    We already checked there are no pure ones, so let's look for equilibria
    where both players use both strategies.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-tennis-pq-2">
    <title>Tennis, with mixed strategies</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1-q</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Receiver: <m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>Receiver: <m>f</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell>Server: <m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>(.4, .6)</cell>
        <cell>(.8, .2)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1-p</m></cell>
        <cell>Server: <m>f</m></cell>
        <cell>(.7, .3)</cell>
        <cell>(.1, .9)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Here's the Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria (Theorem 5.2) again. A
    mixed strategy profile <m>\sigma</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when,
    for every player <m>i</m>:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>s_i</m> and <m>s_i'</m> are both active, then
          <m>\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i}) = \pi_i(s_i', \sigma_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>s_i</m> is active and <m>s_i'</m> is not, then
          <m>\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i}) \geq \pi_i(s_i', \sigma_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    In words: every active strategy earns the same, and nothing unused earns
    more.
  </p>

  <p>
    Since both players use both strategies, there's nothing unused, so only
    condition (1) matters. Let's start with the receiver.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-receiver-in-words">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose both of the receiver's strategies are active. In words, what
        does condition (1) of Theorem 5.2 say about her?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Against the server's mix, anticipating the backhand and
            anticipating the forehand earn her the same expected payoff.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If one earned more, she'd drop the other one. She's only
            willing to mix if she's indifferent.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Her two strategies earn the server the same expected payoff.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Wrong player. Condition (1) is about each player's own payoffs.
            The receiver cares about what her strategies earn <em>her</em>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            She anticipates each side half the time.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Equal payoffs doesn't mean equal probabilities. Condition (1) is
            about what her strategies earn, not how often she uses them.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    In symbols, that's <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, b) = \pi_2(\sigma_1, f)</m>, where
    <m>\sigma_1 = pb + (1-p)f</m>. To compute each side, read down the
    receiver's column using the second numbers, weighted by the server's
    probabilities:
    <md>
      <mrow>.6p + .3(1-p) \amp = .2p + .9(1-p)</mrow>
      <mrow>.3 + .3p \amp = .9 - .7p</mrow>
      <mrow>p \amp = .6</mrow>
    </md>
    Notice that we found the <em>server's</em> <m>p</m> using the
    <em>receiver's</em> payoffs. That's what we saw on the last page: the
    server's mix is what keeps the receiver indifferent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now it's your turn with the server.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-solve-for-q">
    <title>Solving for <m>q</m></title>
    <task xml:id="eg-solve-for-q-equation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Both of the server's strategies are active. Use condition (1) of
          Theorem 5.2 to write an equation in <m>q</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          On the last page you computed <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m> with
          <m>q = .7</m>. Do the same thing, but leave <m>q</m> as a letter.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2) = \pi_1(f, \sigma_2)</m>. Read across
          each of the server's rows using the first numbers, weighted by the
          receiver's probabilities:
          <me>.4q + .8(1-q) = .7q + .1(1-q)</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="eg-solve-for-q-solve">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve your equation for <m>q</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <md>
          <mrow>.8 - .4q \amp = .1 + .6q</mrow>
          <mrow>q \amp = .7</mrow>
        </md>
        <p>
          Same as the last page!
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </example>

  <p>
    So <m>(.6b + .4f,\ .7b + .3f)</m> is the only mixed strategy Nash
    equilibrium where both players use both strategies.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-2">
    <title>Section 5.2, Tennis</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.2.</alert> We just worked out most of this, so you
      can move quickly through the math.
    </p>
    <p>
      Slow down at the end, starting with <q>Note that in the course of
      finding this Nash equilibrium, we actually did more.</q> This is how we
      know we've found all the equilibria.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ORIGINAL (longer) version of the reading block, kept for comparison.

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-2">
    <title>Section 5.2, Tennis</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Above, we used the Fundamental Theorem to find all the mixed Nash
      equilibria where both players use two active strategies. That's most of
      this section, so you can move quickly through the math if you've already
      worked it out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Slow down for the last page, which starts with <q>Note that in the course
      of finding this Nash equilibrium, we actually did more.</q> When we solved
      for <m>p</m>, we only used the fact that the receiver mixes. So if she
      mixes, the server has to mix too. This rules out equilibria where one
      player mixes and the other doesn't, which is how we know we've found
      them all.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  -->

  <p>
    Section 5.3 finds the same equilibrium two other ways. The first uses
    calculus. Since <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> determine everything, we can think
    of the payoffs as functions <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> and <m>\pi_2(p, q)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before reading, let's figure out what the derivatives should do at a Nash
    equilibrium where both players mix. Recall Theorem 5.3: at a Nash
    equilibrium, any mix of a player's active strategies earns her the same
    payoff. We saw this on the last page. With the receiver at
    <m>q = .7</m>, the server gets <m>.52</m> for <em>every</em> <m>p</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-partials-zero">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(p, q)</m> is a Nash equilibrium with <m>0 \lt p \lt 1</m>
        and <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Which is true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial p} = 0</m> and
            <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_2}{\partial q} = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Each player's payoff is flat in her own variable, so its
            derivative is <m>0</m>. Heads up: unlike Chapter 1, a zero
            derivative doesn't mean a peak here. <m>\pi_1</m> is a line in
            <m>p</m>, so a zero derivative means the line is flat.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q} = 0</m> and
            <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_2}{\partial p} = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Tempting, since each player's mix keeps the <em>other</em> player
            indifferent. But the indifference is in each player's own choice:
            the server doesn't care what <m>p</m> she picks. That's a
            statement about <m>\pi_1</m> as <m>p</m> changes.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial p} = 0</m> and
            <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q} = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The server only controls <m>p</m>. If the receiver changes
            <m>q</m>, the server's payoff can certainly change. What Theorem
            5.3 tells us is that the server's own choice of <m>p</m> doesn't
            matter.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-3">
    <title>Section 5.3, Other Ways to Find Mixed Strategy Nash Equilibria</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.3.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      In 5.3.1, notice that <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial p} = .7 - q</m>
      is just <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2) - \pi_1(f, \sigma_2)</m>. So setting it to
      <m>0</m> gives the same equation you solved above. In 5.3.2, spend some
      time with Figure 5.1. Note that <m>p</m> is on the vertical axis.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-receiver-best-response">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The book spells out the server's best response. Now think about the
        receiver. Suppose the server uses some <m>p \lt .6</m>. What is the
        receiver's best response, and why?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q = 0</m>. Since <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_2}{\partial q} \lt 0</m>,
            her payoff goes down as <m>q</m> goes up, so she should always
            anticipate the forehand.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Similarly, if <m>p \gt .6</m> her best response is
            <m>q = 1</m>, and if <m>p = .6</m> any <m>q</m> works.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It depends. If <m>p \gt .5</m> the server mostly serves to the
            backhand, so <m>q = 1</m>. If <m>p \lt .5</m>, then <m>q = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The cutoff is <m>.6</m>, not <m>.5</m>. She returns an anticipated
            forehand much more often than an anticipated backhand (<m>.9</m>
            versus <m>.6</m>), so she keeps anticipating the forehand until
            backhand serves get frequent enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Any <m>q</m>, since she's indifferent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            She's only indifferent at exactly <m>p = .6</m>. For any other
            <m>p</m>, one of her pure strategies is strictly better.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-best-response-intersection">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How do the graphs of <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m> in Figure 5.1 relate to
        the Nash equilibria?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The Nash equilibria are the points where the two graphs intersect.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. At an intersection, each player's strategy is a best
            response to the other's. That's the definition of a Nash
            equilibrium!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Every point on the vertical segment of <m>B_1</m> is a Nash
            equilibrium, since the server is indifferent there.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The server is best responding, but the receiver might not be. At
            <m>(p, q) = (1, .7)</m>, for example, the receiver's best response
            to <m>p = 1</m> is <m>q = 1</m>, not <m>.7</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Any point on either graph is a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            A point on <m>B_1</m> only means the server is best responding. For
            a Nash equilibrium, both players have to be best responding at the
            same time.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
